Assignment 3: Terminal Navigation Practice
Step 1: Open VS Code and the terminal
Open VS Code.
Click Terminal in the top menu.
Select New Terminal.

Your terminal will open at the bottom of the screen.

Step 2: Show your current folder

Type:

pwd

Meaning: Displays your current working directory.

Example output:

Path
----
C:\Users\YourName

Your actual path may be different.

Step 3: Go to your home directory

Type:

cd ~

Meaning: Takes you to your Windows user home directory.
Step 4: Create the website folder and its subfolders

Type:

mkdir website

Now enter the website folder:

cd website

Create the css and images folders:

mkdir css
mkdir images

Your folder structure will now look like this:

website/
├── css/
└── images/
Step 5: Create the HTML and CSS files

First, create index.html:

New-Item index.html -ItemType File

Next, create style.css inside the CSS folder:

New-Item css/style.css -ItemType File

Your folder structure should now look like this:

website/
|─ index.html
├─ css/
│   └-style.css
└-- images/

Step 6: Enter the CSS folder

Type:

cd css

To check your current location, type:

pwd

Your path should end with:

\website\css
Step 7: Return to the website folder

Type:

cd ..

Meaning: Moves you up one directory, from css to website.

To verify, run:

pwd

Your path should now end with:

\website
Step 8: List all files and folders recursively

Type:

ls -Recurse

Meaning: Displays the contents of the website folder, including items inside its subfolders.

You should see index.html, css, style.css, and images.

Step 9: Rename index.html to home.html

Run:

Rename-Item index.html home.html

Now your HTML file is named home.html.

Rename it back:

Rename-Item home.html index.html

Your file is now back to its original name.

Step 10: Open the website folder in VS Code

Type:

code .